# Line endings, settled once.  See tr4w/build/Lint-LineEndings.ps1 for the
# other half of this: .gitattributes governs what git WRITES on checkout and
# STORES on commit, but it cannot stop a tool from writing a file with the
# wrong endings straight into the working tree.  The lint catches that.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.  This was `core.autocrlf=true` and nothing else, which
# is per-machine configuration and is not committed -- so the tree's line
# endings depended on how each clone happened to be configured.  Two things
# went wrong because of it (2026-08-05):
#
#   1. A .pas written with LF endings was opened in the RAD Studio designer.
#      The IDE computes insertion offsets assuming CRLF, so adding an event
#      handler spliced the new code into the MIDDLE of two identifiers
#      (`btnCloseClick(Sende` / `r: TObject)`).  Nothing was deleted; the text
#      landed at the wrong byte offset.  150 tracked .pas files were sitting
#      in the working tree with LF endings at the time, VC.pas among them.
#
#   2. More seriously, the golden-master corpus references (ref.adi/ref.cbr)
#      are BYTE-DIFFED against the D12 export, and git was EOL-converting them
#      -- they were stored LF and checked out CRLF.  They round-tripped only
#      because of one particular autocrlf setting.  A contributor configured
#      differently would get different bytes on disk and see corpus failures
#      that are not in the code at all.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default: detect text, store it LF in the repository, check it out CRLF.
# This is a Windows/Delphi tree; CRLF in the working copy is what every tool
# here expects.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*               text=auto eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sources the RAD Studio IDE co-owns.  These MUST be CRLF in the working tree:
# the IDE edits them by byte offset, and an LF file gets spliced (see above).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.pas           text eol=crlf
*.dpr           text eol=crlf
*.dpk           text eol=crlf
*.inc           text eol=crlf
*.dproj         text eol=crlf
*.bdsproj       text eol=crlf
*.fmx           text eol=crlf
*.dfm           text eol=crlf
*.rc            text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scripts that run under Git Bash.  A CRLF here is not cosmetic: the carriage
# return becomes part of the last token on the line, which breaks shebangs and
# heredoc terminators.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh            text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BYTE-EXACT FIXTURES -- never convert, never normalise.
#
# The golden-master corpus (tr4w/test/corpus) byte-diffs ref.adi/ref.cbr
# against what the app exports.  If git rewrites their endings on checkout,
# the diff reports a regression that does not exist.  `-text` rather than
# `binary` so they stay diffable in git -- when a reference legitimately
# changes we want to read the change, not just see "binary files differ".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.adi           -text
*.cbr           -text
*.trw           -text
*.dat           -text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary.  Marked explicitly rather than left to git's NUL-byte heuristic --
# TRMASTER.DTA and the .trw logs are the regression suite's inputs, and a
# guess is not good enough for those.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.DTA           binary
*.dcu           binary
*.res           binary
*.obj           binary
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
*.bmp           binary
*.ico           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.zip           binary
*.resources     binary
